": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 64, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 107, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "aiq", "title": "Fortune AIQ 50", "year": 2025, "rank": 46, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 218, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 66, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 62, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 244, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--financials", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Financials", "year": 2024, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 73, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 35, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 276, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 29, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 84, "itemDescription": "The insurance provider fell 18 spots on this year\u2019s list as its revenue and profits both declined in 2022 from the year prior.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>The insurance company\u2019s revenue fell to $51 billion in 2022, down 3% from the year prior.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Its profits took a nosedive in 2022. It lost $1.3 billion, a drop of 182% from the previous year. The company reported a net loss of $310 million in the fourth quarter in 2022 alone.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>CEO Tom Wilson blamed the rising cost of auto claims for the company\u2019s losses. Many auto insurance companies struggled as auto claims became more expensive due to inflation and the increased cost of auto parts.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 246, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 66, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The insurance provider increased its revenue to $50.6 billion in 2021, enjoying a 20.7% jump from the previous year. Allstate\u2019s strong growth came as the company reduced costs by divesting itself of $4.4 billion worth of ancillary businesses. The company also boosted its market share in the auto insurance space with its $4 billion purchase of National General. Allstate\u2019s new-business acquisition for the year underperformed somewhat, however, with agents selling 2.5 million policies in 2021, down from 2.6 million the year before.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": "&nbsp;"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 254, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The insurer moves up two spots, after a year in which revenues rose 0.3%, to $44 billion, while profits trended up nicely, rising 15% from 2019. In the first quarter of 2021, Allstate announced that it was divesting some businesses (life and annuity), which resulted in a $4 billion loss in the quarter. Still, adjusted net income (excluding the divestiture) was $1.9 billion, or $6.11 per common share, \u201crepresenting a 23.2% return on equity over the last 12 months,\u201d according to the company. Allstate shareholders have certainly been \u201cin good hands\u201d: The stock has been on a steady upward trajectory from its pandemic lows. The insurer\u2019s odds of climbing higher on the list next year seem assured, given its acquisition of the nearly Fortune-500-size company National General Holdings (No. 535 on our list in 2020), which closed in January 2021.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:737,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:744}\">The insurer moves up nine spots, after a year in which revenues rose 0.3%, to $44 billion, while profits trended up nicely, rising 15% from 2019. In the first quarter of 2021, Allstate announced that it was divesting some businesses (life and annuity), which resulted in a $4 billion loss in the quarter. Still, adjusted net income (excluding the divestiture) was $1.9 billion, or $6.11 per common share, \u201crepresenting a 23.2% return on equity over the last 12 months,\u201d according to the company. Allstate shareholders have certainly been \u201cin good hands\u201d: The stock has been on a steady upward trajectory from its pandemic lows. The insurer\u2019s odds of climbing higher on the list next year seem assured, given its acquisition of the nearly Fortune-500-size company National General Holdings (No. 535 on our list in 2020), which closed in January 2021.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 70, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The insurer moves up two spots, after a year in which revenues rose 0.3%, to $44 billion, while profits trended up nicely, rising 15% from 2019. In the first quarter of 2021, Allstate announced that it was divesting some businesses (life and annuity), which resulted in a $4 billion loss in the quarter. Still, adjusted net income (excluding the divestiture) was $1.9 billion, or $6.11 per common share, \u201crepresenting a 23.2% return on equity over the last 12 months,\u201d according to the company. Allstate shareholders have certainly been \u201cin good hands\u201d: The stock has been on a steady upward trajectory from its pandemic lows. The insurer\u2019s odds of climbing higher on the list next year seem assured, given its acquisition of the nearly Fortune-500-size company National General Holdings (No. 535 on our list in 2020), which closed in January 2021.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:737,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:744}\">The insurer moves up two spots, after a year in which revenues rose 0.3%, to $44 billion, while profits trended up nicely, rising 15% from 2019. In the first quarter of 2021, Allstate announced that it was divesting some businesses (life and annuity), which resulted in a $4 billion loss in the quarter. Still, adjusted net income (excluding the divestiture) was $1.9 billion, or $6.11 per common share, \u201crepresenting a 23.2% return on equity over the last 12 months,\u201d according to the company. Allstate shareholders have certainly been \u201cin good hands\u201d: The stock has been on a steady upward trajectory from its pandemic lows. The insurer\u2019s odds of climbing higher on the list next year seem assured, given its acquisition of the nearly Fortune-500-size company National General Holdings (No. 535 on our list in 2020), which closed in January 2021.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 263, "itemDescription": "Allstate rebounded well in 2019 after the challenges of the previous year, when Hurricane Michael and the historic California wildfires resulted in higher payouts and a 29% decline in profit. Fewer catastrophe claims helped the Northfield Township, Ill. insurer climb 45 spots in the Global 500 rankings this year. Revenues rose 12% year-over-year, to $44.7 billion, while net income, aided by increased premiums, grew a substantial 133%, to $4.7 billion. CEO Tom Wilson has also attributed the profit surge to cost-cutting measures and increased investment in technology. One example of the latter: a new identity-theft protection product called Digital Footprint, which allows customers to view all websites with accounts that have their personal information, and helps them track data breaches that might affect them."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 72, "itemDescription": "Allstate rebounded well in 2019 after the challenges of the previous year, when Hurricane Michael and the historic California wildfires resulted in higher payouts and a 29% decline in profit. Fewer catastrophe claims helped the Northfield Township, Ill., insurer climb 10 spots in the 500 rankings this year. Revenues rose 12% year-over-year, to $44.7 billion, while net income, aided by increased premiums, grew a substantial 133%, to $4.7 billion. CEO Tom Wilson has also attributed the profit surge to cost-cutting measures and increased investment in technology. One example of the latter, a new identity-theft protection product called Digital Footprint, allows customers to view all websites with accounts that have their personal information, and helps them track data breaches that might affect them."}, {"slug": "worl